<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kantō region</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Kanto region</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Kantō region is the largest plain of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honshu">Honshu</a>, now covering Tokyo and six surrounding prefectures. Historically it comprised eight provinces and was the base of the warrior class: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamakura">Kamakura</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a> ruled from there, the Kamakura and Koga kubo governed it for the Ashikaga, and the Later Hojo and then the Tokugawa made it their power base, with Edo as their capital.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kanto-region.jpg" alt="Kantō region" width="320" height="290" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: TUBS · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kanto_Region_in_Japan.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…survived as an ally of Oda Nobunaga and then a vassal of Toyotomi Hideyoshi, accumulating resources in his <b>Kanto</b> domains around Edo while avoiding premature ambition, before defeating the western coalition led by Ishida Mitsunari in the name of Hideyoshi's young heir at the Battle of Sekigahara in…</blockquote><a href="/empire/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa Shogunate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>To control the <b>Kanto</b>, the Ashikaga shogunate created a regional government in Kamakura.</blockquote><a href="/state/kamakura-fu">Kamakura-fu</a></li><li><blockquote>Ashikaga Shigeuji, the restored Kamakura kubo, killed the <b>Kanto</b> deputy Uesugi Noritada in 1454, starting the long Kyotoku War.</blockquote><a href="/state/koga-kubo">Koga kubo</a></li><li><blockquote>Hojo Ujiyasu won a celebrated victory at Kawagoe in 1546 and expanded across the <b>Kanto</b>, resisting attacks by Uesugi Kenshin and Takeda Shingen.</blockquote><a href="/state/later-hojo-clan-domain">Later Hojo clan domain</a></li><li><blockquote>Satake Yoshishige, nicknamed the demon of Hitachi, fought the Later Hojo for control of the northern <b>Kanto</b> and allied with neighbours to resist them.</blockquote><a href="/state/satake-clan-domain">Satake clan domain</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1590 Hideyoshi transferred Ieyasu to the former Later Hojo lands in the <b>Kanto</b>, where he made Edo his capital.</blockquote><a href="/state/tokugawa-clan-domain">Tokugawa clan domain</a></li></ul></section>
